      "id": "ayas",
      "end": 1599,
      "contributors": [],
      "provenance": "Pleiades",
      "associationCertaintyURI": "https://pleiades.stoa.org/vocabularies/association-certainty/certain",
      "start": 1500,
      "details": "<p class=\"p1\">Ay\u0101s:\u00a0It is a port on the <em>Ba\u1e25r-i \u015e\u0101m</em> (the Syrian Sea, i.e., the eastern Mediterranean), belonging to the province of <em>Ermen</em> (Cilician Armenia). From there it is one-day's journey to <em>Tell \u1e24amd\u016bn</em>, and two-day's journey to <em>K\u016bh-\u0131 Ba\u1e33r\u0101\u1e63</em> (Mountain Baqr<em>\u0101</em>s) and its town. By tradition, <em>Ay\u0101s</em> was built by Ay\u0101s, son of Y\u0101v\u0101n, son of Y\u0101fe\u1e6f, son of Noah (peace be upon him). Near it is the <em>\u0130skender\u016bne Bu\u00f1ar\u0131</em> (the Spring of Iskenderun/Alexandria), whose fortress was formerly built by\u00a0<em>Z\u00fcbeyde \u1e2a\u0101t\u016bn-\u0131 \u00dcmm\u00fc Ca\u2018fer</em>. At present, the common people are still mistaken: they call that place the <em>\u0130skenderiyye Bu\u00f1ar\u0131</em> (\u201cSpring of Alexandria\u201d), which is incorrect... [partial translation]</p>",
